# `map`, `filter` and `flatMap`

The three operations that do most of the work.

## `filter` — which elements continue

Takes a **`Predicate`** (element → `boolean`). Keeps the type, count only shrinks.

```java
nums.filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)   // 2, 4, 6 — still Integers, fewer
```

## `map` — what each becomes

Takes a **`Function`** (element → new value). Count unchanged, **type can change**.

```java
names.map(String::length)   // "Ada","Grace" → 3, 5  (Stream<Integer>)
```

*`filter` changes how many; `map` changes what each is.*

## `flatMap` — one element → many, then flatten

`map` a list-producing function and you get a nested **`Stream<List<Item>>`**. `flatMap` maps each element to a **stream**, then concatenates — the nesting disappears:

```java
orders.stream()
    .flatMap(o -> o.items().stream())   // Order → Stream<Item>
    ...                                 // flat Stream<Item>
```

**Rule of thumb:** one value → `map`; many (list, array, `Optional`) → `flatMap`.
